  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${945}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{945}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={585}^{∘} \\\\ {585}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={225}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254214/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${225}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$",
